WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.819
FedRAMP High is connected to NIST security control concepts and applies them

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:06.785
in a cloud authorization context.

00:00:06.785 --> 00:00:11.111
Control families cover areas such as access control, audit and accountability,

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:13.864
configuration management, contingency planning, identification and authentication,

00:00:13.864 --> 00:00:18.189
incident response, risk assessment, system and communications protection, and related domains.

00:00:18.189 --> 00:00:23.695
High-impact use cases demand that those expectations be translated into mature implementation and evidence.

00:00:23.695 --> 00:00:29.200
Control implementation statements matter because they explain how the real service satisfies the expectation.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.346
Strong statements describe architecture, tooling, procedures, ownership, inheritance,

00:00:32.346 --> 00:00:33.919
customer responsibility, and evidence.

00:00:33.919 --> 00:00:38.638
Weak statements merely repeat control language without showing how the system works.

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:39.425
For High,

00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:44.144
unsupported or generic descriptions are especially problematic because reviewers need confidence that

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:46.897
controls operate consistently in a high-impact environment.

00:00:46.897 --> 00:00:49.256
Evidence is the practical proof layer.

00:00:49.256 --> 00:00:53.189
Configuration records, logs, tickets, diagrams, scans, test results, monitoring outputs,

00:00:53.189 --> 00:00:57.908
procedures, and operational records help connect a control claim to assessment conclusions.

00:00:57.908 --> 00:01:02.627
A High baseline raises the importance of consistency between what the documentation

00:01:02.627 --> 00:01:05.379
says and what the system actually does.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:09.705
The evidence should come from real operations, not from after-the-fact storytelling.

00:01:09.705 --> 00:01:12.851
Shared responsibility and inherited controls need careful handling.

00:01:12.851 --> 00:01:17.570
Some controls may come from an underlying platform, some from corporate services,

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:21.503
some from the provider, and some from the agency customer.

00:01:21.503 --> 00:01:23.862
High does not erase that complexity.

00:01:23.862 --> 00:01:27.008
It requires the team to make responsibility explicit,

00:01:27.008 --> 00:01:29.368
trace inherited controls to reliable sources,

00:01:29.368 --> 00:01:33.300
and explain what remains within the cloud service offering boundary.
